--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/backstage/namespace/base/kustomization.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# The portal's namespace, and nothing else.
+#
+# crew#488 / crew#539. Three platform rows -- cluster-state, spire and image-automation -- apply
+# objects into namespace `backstage`: the catalogue collector's ServiceAccount and CronJob, the
+# SPIFFE proof CronJob, the ghcr-pull-reader Role. On a cluster with no history those rows ran
+# before the namespace existed and Flux answered `namespaces "backstage" not found`, which is not
+# a dry-run failure and so sat there unread until the row timed out (portability drill run
+# 33214748124, two of its five ROOT-REDs).
+#
+# The obvious edge -- `dependsOn: [backstage]` -- is the one edge the estate forbids. The portal
+# row carries `wait: true` with a 5m timeout and a healthCheck on Deployment/catalogue, so that
+# edge makes every one of those rows wait on the portal being *healthy*, which is crew#539
+# verbatim: one stalled catalogue rollout held fifteen Kustomizations Not Ready and the founder
+# said "one broken deployment is holding the estate".
+#
+# So the namespace is its own layer. It creates one object, it can never be unhealthy, and the
+# rows that need it depend on that instead of on the portal. `platform/backstage/base` no longer
+# carries the Namespace; the k3d overlay picks this directory up directly, because
+# `make catalogue-deploy` applies it by hand with no Flux to order anything.
+apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
+kind: Kustomization
+resources:
+  - namespace.yaml
